A||||14||LarryB||yet more overclocking ||||1||
Z||000000||LarryB||04-04-2001||01:02 AM||||Here's a snipped screenshot of my latest project:<P>Abit K7A-RAID<BR>Athlon 1.2GHz @ 1.474GHz (11 x 134MHz FSB)<BR>GlobalWin FOP-38 fan<BR>256MB stick Crucial CAS2 SDRAM<BR>GeForce 2 Pro video card (running @ 4x AGP)<BR>Windows 98SE<P>Darned thing even boots into DOS at 1573MHz.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>On the right you can see some rc5 figures as well hehe<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/screen2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Terrybogin||04-04-2001||01:06 AM||||WOW!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||04-04-2001||01:26 AM||||I wanna know, do the drive bay doors flip up at that speed?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000003||loafer87gt||04-04-2001||12:03 PM||||Impressive. I am using an Alpha fan on my Asus A7v133 RAID and I can only reach 1.4 on my Thunderbird 1.2. I have been doing the overclocking just in the bios, which works great but there is a glitch that prevents you from upping the voltage. This weekend I am going to pop my case opne and just manually set the jumpers to give it a bit more juice. How stable is it? Can it run the 3D mark 2001 demo without locking up?||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||04-04-2001||02:01 PM||||I haven't thrown 3dmark at it yet because I don't have it installed on that machine.  It runs the Final Reality demo without a hitch and I've been playing some Quake2 as well.  I originally had a Creative Labs TNT card and although the machine was stable I could see some artifacts in the display so I removed it and swapped in the GeForce card.  Solved that problem nicely. After beating on the machine with Q2 (and running rc5 in the background) the temp goes up to 41-42 degrees C. <P>When I get a chance the next couple of days I'll probably download the 3dmark demo and give it a shot. <P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by loafer87gt:<BR><B>Impressive. I am using an Alpha fan on my Asus A7v133 RAID and I can only reach 1.4 on my Thunderbird 1.2. I have been doing the overclocking just in the bios, which works great but there is a glitch that prevents you from upping the voltage. This weekend I am going to pop my case opne and just manually set the jumpers to give it a bit more juice. How stable is it? Can it run the 3D mark 2001 demo without locking up?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||frontal lobe||04-04-2001||02:06 PM||||Hey, I just picked up an abit KT7A-Raid and a 1 GHz t-bird 200fsb for $220!  I should be getting it any day now.  What multiplier and FSB do you think I should go for?  Or where should I start?  What settings?  Or should I just leave well enough alone and be happy with it as is?<P>Also, are you using more than 1 hard drive?  Is it worth it?||||reg||1
Z||000006||LarryB||04-04-2001||02:38 PM||||You got a good deal on that MB/CPU combo.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>If you're going to overclock make sure you have one of the better AMD-approved fans (pass on the Global Orbs and the like.)<P>If you just want to experiement a bit you can try bumping the FSB a little.  You should be using Cricial or Mushkin memory with an Athlon anyways (IMO).  You're stuck with the 10X multiplier unless you're one of the fortunate ones who gets a factory-unlocked processor or are willing to perform the unlock trick yourself.  You can go to either<BR> <A HREF="http://www.overclockers.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.overclockers.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.athlonmb.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.athlonmb.com</A> <P>and do a bit of research.<P>I'm using 1 IDE drive and 1 SCSI drive (I have a scanner on this box as well) so I'm not taking advantage of the RAID hardware on this machine.  Whether you use it or not depends on your risk-taking ability (and that's pretty much true for overlocking in general).  I have a VP-6 machine where I'm mirroring 2 x 60GB drives but the increased data safeguard is worth the extra $$$ in that case. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>Hey, I just picked up an abit KT7A-Raid and a 1 GHz t-bird 200fsb for $220!  I should be getting it any day now.  What multiplier and FSB do you think I should go for?  Or where should I start?  What settings?  Or should I just leave well enough alone and be happy with it as is?<P>Also, are you using more than 1 hard drive?  Is it worth it?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||LarryB||04-04-2001||02:41 PM||||Duh. Make that "golden orbs" and the like.  GlobalWin FOP-32-1 or FOP-38 are 2 of the more decent heatsinks, the FOP-38 has a higher-capacity fan but its comparatively noisy.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JohnnyK||04-04-2001||03:39 PM||||I remember when I got a 386 16mhz.. Man was I happy..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||04-04-2001||03:53 PM||||HA!  My 486 DX-2 66mhz could leave yours sitting at the start button!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>It has a 300mega-cubeyte HD too. What power!<P>Still have it...<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000010||loafer87gt||04-04-2001||04:25 PM||||You know the real ironic thing is that I still use my 12 year old Amiga 3000 to do some of my more intensive graphics stuff. 25 megahertz and only 16 megs of ram, but an efficient as heck operating system!||||reg||1
Z||000011||frontal lobe||04-04-2001||06:43 PM||||LarryB, I picked up the combo (slightly-like about 1 month) used from a guy.  Found it on the for-sale forum of overclockers.  I don't know how brave I will be with trying to unlock it.  Somebody just had a good review article on it that I read-anandtech or sharkyextreme or Tom's hardware but I can't remember where.||||reg||1
Z||000012||LarryB||04-04-2001||11:39 PM||||It's a good motherboard, I also have a K7-RAID (100 MHz board) and I'm really happy with both of them.  I also have other machines so I'm not dead in the water if I manage to toast one.<P>I figure overclocking is kind of like investing, go with whatever level of risk you feel comfortable with.  <P>BTW one of my friends in Denver is using a 1 GHz Athlon in his gaming machine. His box really kicks serious butt, so don't feel like you'll have a 2nd-rate machine at all!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>LarryB, I picked up the combo (slightly-like about 1 month) used from a guy.  Found it on the for-sale forum of overclockers.  I don't know how brave I will be with trying to unlock it.  Somebody just had a good review article on it that I read-anandtech or sharkyextreme or Tom's hardware but I can't remember where.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Jimmy||04-05-2001||01:47 AM||||I just built a 1200 Athlon with the KT7A Raid system for a buddy of mine.  I couldn't get the sucker to O'C worth a Sh!t.  Right now I have the bus at 142mhz and the multiplier at 8.5.  1207 mhz.  Bench scores are higher with the faster bus  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> , but with what I have been reading on various forums I should be able to get 1300mhz with a 140+ bus speed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> Oh well my buddy isn't to into the whole O'C thing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> he is just tickled that he can play his UT at a ridiculous framerate. hehe... <P>Jim<BR>My CPU specs:<BR>Slot 1 650 Athlon<BR>Biostar crap mobo<BR>256megs of Micron CAS 2 133mhz<BR>Quadro 256 32SDR video card (formerly a GeForce 256, AMAZING what you can do by moving a couple resistors!)<P>Most componets are a 1+yrs old so you know what that means... UPGRADE!||||reg||1
Z||000014||LarryB||04-05-2001||08:23 AM||||There's a lot of spewage and pontification on a lot of the forums.  Or, you can just read the newsgroup alt.comp.hardware.overclocking.amd and see a lot of the same junk.(Although there are some good nuggets to be mined out there.)<P>Some processors just won't OC well, one of my 1200s is unstable over 1263 and won't even boot W2K or Linux at anything over 1284.  It still makes for a great Linux box @ 1200 though, one of these days I'm going to load up either FreeBSD or Solaris x86 w/ipfilter and use it to replace my existing dual-PPro firewall.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jimmy:<BR><B>I just built a 1200 Athlon with the KT7A Raid system for a buddy of mine.  I couldn't get the sucker to O'C worth a Sh!t.  Right now I have the bus at 142mhz and the multiplier at 8.5.  1207 mhz.  Bench scores are higher with the faster bus   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> , but with what I have been reading on various forums I should be able to get 1300mhz with a 140+ bus speed.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> Oh well my buddy isn't to into the whole O'C thing   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> he is just tickled that he can play his UT at a ridiculous framerate. hehe... <P>Jim<BR>My CPU specs:<BR>Slot 1 650 Athlon<BR>Biostar crap mobo<BR>256megs of Micron CAS 2 133mhz<BR>Quadro 256 32SDR video card (formerly a GeForce 256, AMAZING what you can do by moving a couple resistors!)<P>Most componets are a 1+yrs old so you know what that means... UPGRADE!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
